so um so we're here at the wetlands park  and unfortunately multiple times  most years we do have these wildfires  that start up  so this is the first one we've had this  year  i'm told it was approximately 45 acres  in size  and it occurred right behind here in the  nature preserve  so unfortunately a lot of habitat burned  we have a lot of wildlife out here  beavers raccoons  birds so unfortunately i'm sure we lost  some of their their habitats  and then we also lost some some really  nice  trees and and shrubs that really make  the wetlands park special  do we know yet what started the fire so  i don't think they finished their  investigation and we haven't heard yet  but oftentimes these are started by you  know just  just people but it's undetermined at  this time  i know you said 45 acres burned how many  acres is the park  total so the entire park is 2 900 acres  but the nature preserve right here is  only 210 acres so we lost about 25  percent of the nature preserve last  night  were any structures in danger of hitting  fire  so what's what's really great is we have  a nice partnership  with the blm wildland firefighters  and clark county fire department so we  provide them maps for access into all of  these areas we provide them the  locations of structures bridges  shade structures restrooms all of that  so they had that information and they  knew which areas to protect so  thankfully they protected the homes  which is of course the most important  thing  and no other structures were impacted so  they did a really good job  at protecting infrastructure last night  from previous you know burns  and going forward how it affects the  natural uh resource so unfortunately you  know  fires are part of of the ecosystem and  you know a lot more fires are occurring  we've had these every year  at the wetlands park and we have a  partnership with blm nevada division of  forestry national park service  to actually install fuel brakes fuel  reduction programs  and so both of the all of those agencies  are out here working right now to help  reduce the fuel load  so when when these fires do occur we are  prepared to come back in and do some  restoration  afterwards fire is bad for habitat and  wildlife  but then you do get that natural  regeneration so we take advantage of  these opportunities because it does  clear out some of the fuel to go  in and plant so you know it's it's it's  it's devastating for the habitat but it  does provide opportunities for us to  improve uh the landscape so that we have  fuels out there that that won't quite  carry the fire like this again  anything else you'd like to add just you  know  you know the wind didn't probably help  things i'm sure  so actually i was talking with the fire  department and  fortunately you know this didn't happen  a few days earlier  and because the winds actually shifted  in direction  they were able to keep um they're able  to do some back burning  and move the the fuel away from the  houses so actually it worked in their  favor last night and that doesn't always  happen  okay now we're seeing people out here  but are  some of the trails here at the uh  the nature preserve are they closed now  so when things like this happen  um so last night fortunately the park  was already closed  so they were able to do the work that  they needed to do  um today they they're temporarily  closing  certain areas just as you know they have  little flare-ups  and whatnot but most of the trails are  open but if anybody does come out today  they just need to be cautious  and you know stay on the trails and not  go into the burn  you said a lot of the areas were damaged  by the fire are they going to be able to  recover  or will they just have to start new ones  in some of the areas that weren't burned  so it's kind of interesting  we've been studying this over the years  that some  plants don't recover but other plants  do so we noticed in the fire that we had  last year about this time that the  mesquites were able to re-sprout  and same thing with some of the  cottonwoods but if the fire gets too  intense  it actually does kill the trees and then  last night you know some of the the  photos were pretty dramatic  and you get that really high flame  length and that's from the  the reeds that are out here and so reeds  are actually they're not native  to this particular park and so  um it's it was kind of worked out to  clear out that fuel load and then we can  come back in and plant trees  so it's it's a mixed bag  what kind of activity can you do here at  the woodlands park  does that affect anybody so  you know over the last year there's been  lots of changes of course  we've had a near 100  increase in visitation out here a lot of  people have been fighting their parks  over the last year  and so here at the wetlands park our  trails are open every day dawn to dusk  there's  bicycling trails there's wildlife  viewing bird  watching there's so much that you can do  out here just to connect with nature and  so  unfortunately this wetlands park burned  last night but it really does  you know bring it back to the forefront  that this is a really wonderful  park that you can come out and with your  family and friends and enjoy  nature in a safe environment  and unfortunately you know our exhibit  gallery is closed right now for obvious  reasons but typically we do have an  indoor facility where you can come in  and see interactive exhibits we offer  programs year-round  right now we're offering virtual  programs only we offer a virtual  field trip we have a wetlands discovery  club  where you can do activities at home and  then come out into the park  and do activities here as well we also  have  water coloring programs and so if you go  to our website at ccwetlandspark.org you  can see  all kinds of information about the park  and ways to  get involved out here  thank you very much
 

